<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bar-Rakib</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">fl. c. 730 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century-bce">8th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bar-Rakib was a king of Sam'al, an Aramaean kingdom in south-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a>, in the later 8th century BCE. A loyal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Assyrian king Tiglath-Pileser III, he set up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aramaic">Aramaic</a> inscriptions honouring his father Panamuwa II and boasting of his own palace building. His reliefs, including one showing him enthroned before a scribe, are among the best-known finds from the site at Zincirli.</p></div>
